   "PAPER:Structural_Influence_published"
  ],
  "summary": "The glossary defines 'Organizer', 'Shaper', and 'Terminal Scorer' roles based on the 'Structural Influence' paper. This prior work establishes the methodology for deriving these roles from player usage and structural influence. The current question extends this by examining how these roles manifest within specific lineups, their independence from simpler talent metrics, and how teams manage them."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The derivation of 'structural influence' and the subsequent classification into 'Organizer', 'Shaper', and 'Terminal Scorer' roles are complex and depend on the reliability of the underlying play-type classification and the structural fingerprint calculation. Errors in these foundational steps could propagate."
  },
  {
   "code": "OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL",
   "text": "The structural roles themselves are derived labels based on calculated player metrics (usage, structural influence). The validity of the findings hinges on the accuracy and robustness of these derived labels."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "While the overall dataset (420,993 possessions, 90 team-seasons) is substantial, specific unique 5-player lineup combinations may have limited minutes/possessions, especially within a single team. This could reduce the power to robustly estimate role coverage variation or 'talent control' patterns for less common lineups."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "When assessing separability from 'talent/counts' and how 'talent control behaves,' there is a risk of confounding. Player roles are often correlated with inherent talent. Lineup decisions are influenced by numerous factors (opponent, game situation, injuries, player availability), making causal inference challenging without careful controls."
  },
  {
   "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
   "text": "The question implies multiple hypotheses (variation, separability, behavior of talent control). Without proper adjustment for multiple comparisons, there's an increased risk of false positives."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.5,
  "rejected": 0.2,
  "untestable": 0.1,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.1
 },
 "decision": "proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "1. For 'lineup coverage varies within team': The distribution of (number of Organizers, Shapers, Terminal Scorers) tuples across distinct 5-player lineups for each team, and the variance of this distribution. 2. For 'separable from talent/counts': The unique explanatory power (e.g., R-squared change in regression, or information gain) of lineup structural role coverage in predicting lineup performance or composition, beyond what traditional player talent metrics (e.g., total points, assists, usage counts, or aggregated box-score stats) provide. 3. For 'talent control behaves': Patterns in how teams adjust lineup structural role coverage based on player availability (e.g., injuries, rest), opponent characteristics, and game context, as well as how individual player roles shift in response to changes in surrounding teammates.",
  "unit": "The primary unit of analysis will be the lineup-possession. For 'talent control behaves', aggregated lineup-stints or player-game observations may also be used.",
  "sample": "All regular season games from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and the complete portions of 2025-26. Playoffs data may be included if deemed sufficiently representative and not introducing undue confounding. Possessions identified as 'garbage time' will be excluded. A minimum possession threshold (e.g., 50 possessions) will be applied to any specific 5-player lineup to ensure robust role and performance estimation.",
  "power": "With 90 team-seasons and over 400,000 possessions, there should be sufficient power to detect overall patterns of lineup coverage variation and separability from talent at the team and league levels. Power for specific, rarely used lineups may be limited, requiring aggregation or a focus on frequently used combinations. Minimum sample sizes for lineup inclusion will be pre-specified.",
  "controls": "For all analyses: game context variables (score differential, period, time remaining, home/away), opponent strength. For 'separability': Player-level talent metrics (e.g., usage rate, per-game box score stats like points, assists, rebounds, advanced metrics like EPM/LEBRON if available), team-level talent. For 'talent control': Player availability (inactive players, injury reports), opponent structural tendencies, coaching changes, and team strategy.",
  "falsification": "1. If all observed lineups within a team exhibit highly similar structural role coverage (low variance). 2. If structural role coverage provides no statistically significant additional explanatory power for lineup outcomes or composition beyond simpler talent metrics. 3. If no consistent or predictable patterns emerge in how teams adjust structural role coverage under varying conditions (e.g., absence of a key player, facing specific opponent types).",
  "multiplicity": "Multiple hypothesis tests will be conducted across the three parts of the research question. A Bonferroni correction or False Discovery Rate (FDR) control will be applied to p-values for families of related tests to mitigate the risk of Type I errors. Hierarchical modeling approaches will be considered to model variations within teams and across seasons, implicitly handling some multiplicity.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypotheses will be considered supported if statistical tests yield p-values below a pre-specified alpha level (e.g., 0.05) after multiplicity adjustment, and observed effect sizes are deemed practically significant. For 'talent control behaves,' qualitative assessment of consistent patterns and trends will also be considered alongside statistical significance.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, and 2025-26 (complete portions as of 2026-07-13) regular season and playoffs. Play-by-play and derived play-type possession records will be used for role calculation. Rotation, matchups, and summary files will be used for lineup identification and control variables."
 }
}

